#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""apply_batch19_step5.py - Step 5: the three findings that had no tests.

These were reported in the original review and deliberately left until last,
because none of them had a failing test to prove it. All three do now.

  app/services/events.py    The event limit was count_documents then
                            insert_one, with nothing in between, so two
                            concurrent saves could both pass the same check.
                            The decision now happens AFTER the insert, where
                            the count cannot be stale. ObjectIds sort by
                            creation, so every concurrent request agrees on
                            who is over the line: whoever inserted first keeps
                            their event and only the losers roll themselves
                            back. No counter document, so nothing can drift
                            out of step with the events and lock a user out.

  app/services/auth.py      A PUBLIC_SCOPE with its own budget, keyed by
                            client IP, for routes that have no initData to
                            identify the caller. X-Forwarded-For is
                            client-supplied and therefore spoofable - that is
                            a ceiling on what this can do, not a reason to
                            skip it. Also bounds the in-memory fallback store:
                            it kept one permanent entry per identity, which is
                            harmless for Telegram ids and a slow leak once the
                            key is an IP address.

  app/routes/share.py       /c/{token} and /c/{token}/card.png now go through
                            that limiter. public_card had no Request object at
                            all, so it gains one.

  app/middleware.py         X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, HSTS on
                            https, Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy, and a CSP
                            derived from what the templates actually load. The
                            policy can be overridden or emptied through
                            CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY on the host, because a
                            policy wrong by one directive shows up as a blank
                            Mini App and whoever is looking at that needs a
                            fix without a deploy.

  templates/countdown.html  Its inline <style> and <script> moved to
                            static/countdown.css and static/countdown.js. The
                            policy has no 'unsafe-inline', so they had to go -
                            and the inline script had been interpolating
                            miniapp_url straight into a JS string literal,
                            which Jinja does not autoescape for. It now
                            arrives through a data attribute.

X-Frame-Options and frame-ancestors are deliberately NOT set. A Telegram Mini
App is loaded inside Telegram's own frame, so denying framing would be the
textbook clickjacking control and would also stop the product opening at all.
Naming the Telegram origins was considered and rejected: the set differs by
client and version, and getting it wrong fails closed on someone's phone where
no console warning would ever be seen.

Eight tests come with this, and every one was checked against a deliberately
broken version first - including one that exists only because the first
rate-limit test proved the limiter works without proving the routes call it.

Usage:
    python apply_batch19_step5.py --check   # resolve everything, write nothing
    python apply_batch19_step5.py           # apply

Existing files are replaced whole and guarded by the hash of their Step 4
version; the three new files are only created if they are not already there.
Reads and writes are UTF-8 with LF, through a temp file and an atomic replace.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def read(path: Path) -> str:
    raw = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    return raw.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n")


def write_atomic(path: Path, content: str) -> None:
    tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + ".tmp")
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as handle:
        handle.write(content)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def sha(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]


